db/ref 17592186564396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564472 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564472 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564472 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-2$ and $f(b)=5,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17eec8d8-70be-5d2a-8c5e-928ea708ee3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564472 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 5-(-2) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨0,2,3⟩-⟨1,0,2⟩) \\\\ 7 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 7 & =-{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=-7.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564490 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-1,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=6$ and $f(b)=2,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "602e236f-f7ee-52d9-b1ca-e9d90f7ae370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564490 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 2-6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\ -4 & ={f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564492 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6547ba2d-be83-5b17-a5ce-a14f2d6a7139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564492 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,7)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,7⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ ({7}^{2}-{3}^{2})-({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =-6{c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 13 & =-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{7-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{6} & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y-1 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}=13,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+4(2{c}_{1}+1) & =13 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{1}+4 & =13 \\\\ 6{c}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}+1=4.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564507 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564507 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=xy$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564507 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90802828-69ad-5b22-b55e-e0d3346dd2ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564507 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=xy$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ 2(4)-0(0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1} \\\\ 4 & ={c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{4-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}=8,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 4{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}=2.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564514 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-3$ and $f(b)=5,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a261cf1f-d1e0-58d8-b832-e9ddda3c5b83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564514 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 5-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2⟩-⟨2,5⟩) \\\\ 8 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-7⟩ \\\\ 8 & ={f}_{x}(c)-7{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
